    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d227">

                <pb facs="0448" n="402" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg402"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.52/302½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-08">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">8 March, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I should like to take
                    time to think this over maturely. I have now no comment to make except this
                    preliminary one: that there seems to me to be a danger that the attempt to
                    disperse the Japanese in this country might lead to the very thing we wish to
                    prevent. It might produce uneasiness in the States to which the Japanese were
                    induced to migrate and to popular clamors there which legislatures might not
                    withstand. Would we not before-hand have to canvass the matter with State
                    authorities and work out a programme which we could be sure from the outset we
                    could carry out. Otherwise the offense might grow greater, not less, in case of
                    failure.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
